WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.678
Iterator, reference, and pointer invalidation is one of the most important standard-library safety topics.

2
00:00:05.678 --> 00:00:12.493
Containers make ownership easier, but access into a container is still governed by each container's mutation rules.

3
00:00:12.493 --> 00:00:19.685
Operations such as insert, erase, resize, reserve, clear, and reallocation can invalidate iterators, references, pointers, and non-owning views.

4
00:00:19.685 --> 00:00:27.256
The exact rules vary by container and operation, so reviewers should check the specific contract rather than relying on memory.

5
00:00:27.256 --> 00:00:31.042
A vector reallocation can make existing pointers and references stale.

6
00:00:31.042 --> 00:00:36.342
Erasing from a sequence can invalidate the erased element and sometimes elements after it.

7
00:00:36.342 --> 00:00:39.370
Clearing a container invalidates access to its elements.

8
00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:43.156
Strings can have similar issues when their internal buffer changes.

9
00:00:43.156 --> 00:00:45.427
Long-lived access is review-sensitive.

10
00:00:45.427 --> 00:00:53.756
Cached iterators, references stored in member variables, pointers into vectors or strings, and views returned to callers all require a clear lifetime

11
00:00:53.756 --> 00:00:54.134
story.

12
00:00:54.134 --> 00:00:59.056
If the source container can mutate, the cached access may stop being safe.

13
00:00:59.056 --> 00:01:00.570
Erase loops deserve care.

14
00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:10.034
Code should use the iterator returned by erase when that is the appropriate pattern, or rebuild the range in a way that makes invalidation obvious.

15
00:01:10.034 --> 00:01:14.577
Hidden assumptions in loop updates are a common source of maintenance defects.

16
00:01:14.577 --> 00:01:18.741
The safe habit is to discard or refresh access after mutation.

17
00:01:18.741 --> 00:01:25.176
When code mutates a container, ask which existing iterators, references, pointers, spans, or string_views are still valid.

18
00:01:25.176 --> 00:01:29.341
If the answer is uncertain, shorten the lifetime of that access.

19
00:01:29.341 --> 00:01:34.641
Tests can help catch invalidation mistakes, especially with debug iterators or sanitizers where available.

20
00:01:34.641 --> 00:01:41.076
But the design should make invalidation assumptions visible enough that reviewers can reason about them before runtime.

21
00:01:41.076 --> 00:01:46.755
APIs should avoid returning long-lived access into containers unless the lifetime contract is simple.

22
00:01:46.755 --> 00:01:55.840
Returning an index, key, copied value, or short-lived view may be safer than returning a reference that callers might store across later mutations.
